# `core/alloc`'s `scoped`, and every way a value gets out of one.
#
# A scope's pages go back when its body returns, so a value that leaves has to
# be a copy the scope is not about to unmap. There are two doors and they are
# not the same door: `copyOut`, which copies onto **whichever allocator is
# active** and is what `scoped` itself uses on the way out; and `copyAcross`,
# which leaves every arena first and is what `core/actor` uses at each of its
# four crossings into the runtime.
#
# Every value here is one the *program* built rather than a literal, and it is
# `LOOSE` bytes long so that its mapping is unmapped rather than pooled; the
# `churn` between the scope and the read takes the pooled pages back too. Both
# halves are needed, and `conformance/lib/actor/test/scoped.buri`'s header is
# where the argument is written out in full. Here they are through the CLI, in
# a repository, on whichever backend the invocation asks for — which on a
# toolchain with a native backend is where there is an arena to get wrong.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.
#
# **This case is the one run in the repository that leaks**, and it is listed by
# name in cli/tests/harness/mod.rs's KNOWN_LEAKS: the last block below leaves a
# message in an actor's mailbox for `stop` to discard, and a discarded message's
# payload is released by nobody. The leak is `core/actor`'s and not this case's
# — it reproduces with no scope in sight, and `build::heap` provokes it on
# purpose and pins the number — so the run below is made again without the heap
# check and the goldens record what the case was written to assert. The day the
# defect is fixed, `build::heap` goes red and says to delete both.

doc: "a value built inside a scope survives the scope, at every crossing a program can reach"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/vault", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "pass.txt"
}

# The negative twin. A retired scope is not a use-after-free waiting to happen
# and not an error either — it is a handle that matches no arena, so what it is
# asked for it answers and what it is charged stops moving. Flipping the stop's
# expectation records the answer an escaped handle really gives.
edit {
  file: "lib/vault/test/crossings.buri"
  replace: "assert.eq(out.address.stop(out.scope), .Ok(()));\n    assert.eq(out.address.send(out.scope, .Put(\"late\")), .Err(.Stopped));"
  with: "assert.eq(out.address.stop(out.scope), .Err(.Stopped));"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/vault", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "retired.txt"
}
